5. Safe to share, easy to manage.

## Analysis:

I read the core idea doc, background.md, the YouTube series design, the live-demo plan and the register form on avarile.com. Here's my view on go-to-market.

The core idea

"Your AI should know your life (or your company), and you should own that memory." The pitch has three parts:

- Memory: structured data you own.
- Processing: any model can read and write it.
- Learning: closed loops that make it better every week.

What you're really selling is the compounding record, not the software. That's what sets you apart from the incumbents. Notion AI, ChatGPT memory and Claude Projects all promise an "AI that
knows you", but that memory is locked to one platform, unstructured and opaque. Yours is portable, structured and auditable. Lead with that contrast.

The biggest GTM problem: the method and the stack pull against each other

The doc says it's "as lean as possible: only Claude and the database". In reality you run four self-hosted forks (Teable, Plane, Twenty and LibreChat) on one K3s node with about 5.7 GiB of
RAM free. That works for you, but:

- Your normal-people audience (the YouTube series is aimed at "no tech skills") can't self-host. If you host their data, you become a data custodian. That breaks "your data on your own
  database", and it brings privacy, ops and support work.
- The "10-minute template" promised in videos 2 and 3 only holds up if it runs on something people already have.

My recommendation: separate the method from the stack, in the same way you separate data from the model.

- Free and personal tier: publish the method, schema and skills as a template that runs on a mainstream hosted database with an official MCP connector (Teable cloud, Airtable or Notion;
  check which has the most reliable connector). "Storage agnostic" fits your own principle.
- Paid and team tier: the full Cybernetics stack (Data Centre, Projects, CRM and Agents), deployed for clients who want it. The live demo, "Lead to Delivery, Live", is a sales asset for
  this segment, not for the YouTube audience.

